desc:GFX G Sun [Arcana synth]
//tags: instrument synth drone tanpura raga generative harmony arpeggiator pitch detection delay chorus ambient arcana
//author: JGWizrad / Garden FX (GFX)
//
// Requires gfx-drift, gfx-output, gfx-ui, gfx-panel, gfx-instrument and gfx-voice (.jsfx-inc) in the same folder.
//
// THE SUN (card XIX of the GFX Arcana series): radiance, clarity, the unmoving centre that everything turns about.
// A solar drone instrument: an algorithmic TANPURA at the centre, a LISTEN mode that re-tunes its root (Sa) to the
// music on a clock, and seven RINGS OF LIGHT - generative harmony layers radiating from Sa, each with its own scale,
// transposition, pattern and envelope - all into an atmospheric chain (delay with SMEAR, chorus, WIDTH, a tail).
// It plays by itself once PLAY is lit: locked to REAPER's transport while it plays, free-running at the project tempo
// while stopped. It loads silent (PLAY off); TRANSPORT (lit by default) presses PLAY when REAPER plays or records and
// releases it when REAPER stops; PLAY still works by hand. Off, PLAY ignores the transport. Presets leave both alone.
//   TANPURA    four (or five) plucked strings, played in turn 1 - 2 - 3 - 4 for ever. Each string is a physical
//              model (a Karplus-Strong waveguide) that rests on a curved JAWARI bridge: the string grazes the bridge,
//              which buzzes, and a bright, shimmering cloud of upper partials blooms within a tenth of a second of
//              each pluck, then drifts slowly - a little differently every time (FATE breathes it too).
//              PLAY (in the title bar) starts / stops the Sun - the tanpura and the rings (the strings ring on and
//              fade; the rings' notes release). Off by default. TRANSPORT beside it: see above.
//              TUNING (drop-down): PA-SA-SA-SA (the lower 5th, Sa, Sa, low Sa), MA-SA-SA-SA (the lower 4th),
//              NI-SA-SA-SA (the major 7th below Sa), SA-SA-SA-SA, and two five-string tunings PA-NI-SA-SA-SA and
//              MA-NI-SA-SA-SA. JAWARI: the buzz and the cloud's amount (on a slow taper, even to the ear) and
//              brightness. BRIGHTNESS: the pluck and the string's tone.
//              DECAY: how long a string rings (2 - 30 s). RATE: the time between plucks, free (0.3 - 6 s) or SYNC to
//              a division (1/8 .. 2 bars). HUMANISE: the plucks scatter a little late and a little softer.
//              LEVEL, PAN SPREAD (the strings fanned across the stereo field, 1 left .. 4 right).
//              ENVELOPE (AMP / FILTER keys pick which one the four knobs set), applied to each pluck; the gate is one
//              pluck interval: AMP ATTACK, DECAY, SUSTAIN, RELEASE (at its top, HOLD: no release - the Init setting,
//              where the strings simply ring and decay); TONE: CUTOFF (a gentle 12 dB low-pass; at its top and with
//              no envelope it is out of the way), RESO (gentle), ENV (the FILTER envelope's depth, +/- 5 octaves)
//              and the FILTER envelope's ATTACK, DECAY, SUSTAIN, RELEASE.
//              The jawari also carries a slow, fine detune at all times: each string swings 0.8 - 3.8 cents (with
//              JAWARI) at about 0.13 Hz, each at its own rate and phase, so they beat gently against each other.
//   LISTEN     where Sa comes from. SOURCE: MIDI (the LOWEST key held sets Sa's pitch class; it is remembered when
//              the keys are let go), AUDIO (the plug-in's inputs 3 / 4, the sidechain, summed to mono: a YIN pitch
//              detector on a decimated copy, gated by GATE, votes each confident pitch into twelve pitch-class bins
//              through the listening window; the strongest wins) or OFF (ROOT and OCTAVE set Sa directly; a change
//              glides at once). EVERY: Sa is re-tuned only on the clock divider - at the bar line every 1, 2, 4, 8 or
//              16 bars - and without a click: the ringing strings glide to the new tuning (and are damped a little
//              as they go), the next plucks use it, and every ring moves to the new centre at its next note.
//              OCTAVE places Sa (C3 by default in octave 3; Sa is kept within a fourth / tritone of its octave's C).
//              The display shows Sa now and the root that is waiting (NEXT) and how many bars until it lands.
//   RINGS      seven generative voices (three notes each at most; a new note takes the quietest releasing one and
//              fades it out in 8 ms). Pick a ring to edit with its number key, a click on its circle on the display,
//              or the keys 1 - 7; the strip under the display shows all seven (on, scale, pattern, level).
//              Each ring: ON, LEVEL, ATTACK, DECAY, SUSTAIN, RELEASE, TIMBRE (a soft sine pad .. a warm FM glow .. a
//              bright struck bell), PAN; SCALE (GLOBAL or its own: Major, Dorian, Phrygian, Lydian, Mixolydian,
//              Aeolian, Locrian, Harmonic minor, the raga sets Bhairav, Yaman, Kafi, Bhairavi - which share their
//              notes with the thaat's mode where there is one - Pentatonic major and minor); NOTES: how many scale
//              tones it cycles through (1 - 7); TRANSPOSE: DEGREE mode, scale degrees -14 .. +14 from Sa; INTERVAL
//              mode, a fixed interval its notes move in parallel (unison, minor / major 3rd, 4th, 5th, minor / major
//              6th, octave); PATTERN: UP, DOWN, UP/DOWN, AS PLAYED (the keys held, in the order played, in the ring's
//              register; with none held it runs outward from its start), CHORD (a stack of thirds from its tones,
//              three at most), EVERY OTHER, EVERY THIRD, RANDOM; RATE: 4 bars .. 1/16 (each note sounds for 90 % of
//              its step, then releases).
//              HARMONY (all rings): GLOBAL SCALE; ABOVE / BELOW: how many rings radiate upward from Sa (rings 1 .. n)
//              and how many downward (the rest) - an upward ring starts TRANSPOSE degrees above the ring centre (Sa,
//              an octave above the tanpura) and climbs, a downward one mirrors it below and descends; DEGREE /
//              INTERVAL; GENERATION (the chance each step sounds: density); VELOCITY (the scatter of the rings'
//              note velocities); KEY VEL (how much the held keys' velocity sets the rings' loudness); LEVEL.
//   DELAY      synced (1/16 .. 1 bar), FEEDBACK, SMEAR (four all-passes a side in the feedback path: each repeat is
//              blurred further, into a cloud; the first echo stays clear), TONE, MIX. IN lit = in.
//   CHORUS     a rich ensemble (three voices a side): RATE, DEPTH, MIX. IN.
//   WIDTH      mid / side: the side signal from 0 to 200 %, mono below about 150 Hz. IN.
//   TAIL       a gentle four-line reverb tail: SIZE, MIX. IN.
//   FATE       the series' drift (0 - 100 %, 50 % = the standard amount), here the solar breath: the jawari's buzz,
//              each string's tuning and the plucks' timing breathe like a player's, and each ring's brightness swells
//              and recedes on its own slow tide.
//   VOLUME, LEVEL, SAT (the output soft clip is always on).
// MIDI: notes (LISTEN, AS PLAYED and KEY VEL; channel select), CC120 / CC123 all notes off. MIDI passes through.

import gfx-drift.jsfx-inc
import gfx-output.jsfx-inc
import gfx-ui.jsfx-inc
import gfx-panel.jsfx-inc
import gfx-instrument.jsfx-inc
import gfx-voice.jsfx-inc

